<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Tai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taishan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Tai is a mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, rising to about 1,530 metres, the most revered of China's five sacred peaks. Emperors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a> onward performed the feng and shan sacrifices there to announce their mandate to heaven, and its slopes are covered with temples and inscriptions. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-tai.jpg" alt="Mount Tai" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:50304-Taishan_(49055660366).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
